---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { DISCORD_URL } from '../../lib/links';
---

<Base title="Bot privacy policy" description="What the FrightWorks Discord bot sees, keeps and doesn't do.">
  <section class="wrap page">
    <h1 class="display">Bot privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="updated">Last updated November 26, 2026</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The FrightWorks bot is a helper in the <a href={DISCORD_URL}>FrightWorks Discord server</a>. It's run by the
        FrightWorks developers as part of an unofficial fan project.
      </p>

      <h2>What the bot sees</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The commands you use and what you type into them.</li>
        <li>Files you attach to a command, such as <code>console.log</code> with <code>/log</code>.</li>
        <li>
          Basic Discord information that comes with any command: your user ID, username and the channel or post you used
          it in.
        </li>
        <li>
          Activity in the server's forums (when posts are created, tagged or replied to), so it can close solved posts and
          remind people about quiet ones. It doesn't read the text of other messages.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What it does with it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Logs you send with <code>/log</code> are read in memory to find your version, system and known problems, then
          discarded. They aren't saved. A <code>console.log</code> can include your Windows username in file paths and
          details about your PC: the bot's reply only shows you unless you choose to post it, and user folder names are
          hidden in anything posted publicly.
        </li>
        <li>
          When a moderator uses <strong>Send to GitHub</strong> on a bug report, the post's text, its tags and a link back
          to it are copied into a public issue on the FrightWorks GitHub. Your Discord display name may appear in that
          issue. Ask a moderator if you want it removed.
        </li>
        <li>
          The bot keeps a small record linking forum posts to GitHub issues (post ID and issue number) so it can update the
          post when the issue is fixed.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What it doesn't do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>It doesn't sell or share your information, show ads, or track you outside the FrightWorks server.</li>
        <li>It doesn't accept or store game files or disc images.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions and removal requests</h2>
      <p>
        Ask a Moderator in the <a href={DISCORD_URL}>FrightWorks Discord</a>, or open an issue in the <a href="https://github.com/FrightWorks/FrightWorks-no100f/issues">issue tracker</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        This policy may change as the bot does. Changes are posted on this page. See also the <a href="/bot/terms/">bot terms of service</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-block: 56px 96px;
  }
  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    font-size: clamp(2.75rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    color: var(--lantern);
  }
  .updated {
    margin: 0 0 24px;
    color: var(--faint);
  }
</style>
